/*!
 * \page using_configuration How to use the configuration singleton (gum::Configuration)
 *
 * gum::Configuration is a thread-safe, typed and documented key/value store organised in
 * <em>sections</em>. A single global instance, gum::Configuration::instance(), is shared by aGrUM and
 * pyAgrum (<tt>pyagrum.config</tt> is a thin layer above it). It is meant for parameters that a user may
 * want to change <em>without recompiling</em>, by editing a small text file (<tt>agrum.ini</tt>), and that
 * the library, or the user's code, can read or react to.
 *
 * # Where the values come from #
 *
 * -# <b>The defaults of aGrUM</b> are in <tt>src/agrum/base/core/configuration/defaults.ini</tt>. This file is
 *    embedded in the library at build time (it is not read from the disk at run time).
 *    It declares every section and key of aGrUM, its default value, its type and its documentation.
 * -# <b>The defaults of pyAgrum</b> (pyAgrum only) are in <tt>wrappers/pyagrum/pyLibs/defaults.ini</tt>. When
 *    pyAgrum is compiled, cmake concatenates this file after the one of aGrUM before embedding them: pyAgrum
 *    thus creates its own sections and keys. As the result is a single file, the one of pyAgrum must not
 *    reopen a section of the one of aGrUM (this is a gum::SyntaxError when the library is loaded).
 * -# <b>The user file</b>, if it exists in the <em>current directory</em>, overrides some of these values.
 *    Its name is <tt>agrum.ini</tt> for aGrUM, <tt>pyagrum.ini</tt> for pyAgrum (which does not read
 *    <tt>agrum.ini</tt>), see gum::Configuration::userFileName(). It can only change the value of existing
 *    keys: an unknown section or key is reported by a notice and not created. It only needs to contain the
 *    keys to change:
 * \code{.ini}
 * [core]
 * maxNumberOfThreads = 4
 * jgumIndent = 2
 * \endcode
 *
 * The first call to gum::Configuration::instance() builds the configuration from the defaults, then loads
 * the user file. This call is also made when the library is loaded, so the configuration is applied
 * at start-up, before any user code runs.
 *
 * # The syntax of defaults.ini #
 *
 * The file is a classical <tt>.ini</tt> file (<tt>[section]</tt>, <tt>key = value</tt>, comments
 * starting with <tt>#</tt> or <tt>;</tt>). Every key is documented by a <em>metadata line</em> placed
 * immediately above it:
 * \code{.ini}
 * [core]
 * ### int :: default maximum number of threads used by aGrUM (-1 = adapted to the machine)
 * maxNumberOfThreads = -1
 * ### bool :: allow to fix syntax errors in a model instead of raising when exporting
 * allow_modification_when_saving = False
 * \endcode
 *
 * where <tt>type</tt> is one of
 * - <tt>int</tt>, <tt>float</tt>, <tt>bool</tt>, <tt>str</tt>,
 * - an enumeration <tt>v1|v2|...</tt>,
 * - a deprecation <tt>deprecated -> section.key</tt>: the key is kept for compatibility, every access to
 *   it is redirected to <tt>section.key</tt> (and a notice is queued, see below).
 *
 * A line starting with exactly three <tt>#</tt> is a metadata line; <tt>####</tt> and a plain <tt>#</tt> are
 * ordinary comments. A malformed metadata line, a metadata line which is not immediately followed by its
 * key, a duplicated section or key, a default value which does not respect its own type... all raise a
 * gum::SyntaxError (or a gum::InvalidArgument) as soon as the library is loaded: <tt>defaults.ini</tt> is a
 * file maintained by the developers, so it fails fast.
 *
 * A key without metadata line is accepted and treated as an undocumented <tt>str</tt>.
 *
 * Section names are case-sensitive, key names are not.
 *
 * # Reading and writing values #
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * #include <agrum/base/core/configuration/configuration.h>
 *
 * auto& conf = gum::Configuration::instance();
 *
 * std::string s  = conf.get("core", "jgumIndent");                         // "-1"
 * auto        n  = conf.getTyped< std::int64_t >("core", "maxNumberOfThreads");
 * bool        ok = conf.getTyped< bool >("core", "allow_modification_when_saving");
 *
 * conf.set("core", "jgumIndent", "2");    // checked against the declared type
 * \endcode
 *
 * - Values are stored as strings and are <em>canonicalized</em> when set: any accepted spelling of a boolean
 *   (<tt>true/1/on/yes</tt>, <tt>false/0/off/no</tt>, whatever the case) is stored as <tt>True</tt> or
 *   <tt>False</tt>; an enumerated value is stored with the casing declared in <tt>defaults.ini</tt>.
 * - gum::Configuration::getTyped() accepts <tt>bool</tt>, <tt>int64_t</tt>, <tt>double</tt> (also valid for an
 *   <tt>int</tt> key) and <tt>std::string</tt> (valid for any key). Another type is a
 *   gum::InvalidArgument.
 * - gum::Configuration::getValue() gives the value converted according to the declared type of its key
 *   (a gum::Configuration::Value: <tt>bool</tt>, <tt>int64_t</tt>, <tt>double</tt> or <tt>std::string</tt>),
 *   when the type is not known at compile time. This is what pyAgrum uses for <tt>config.typed</tt>.
 * - gum::Configuration::describe() gives a human-readable summary: the differences with the defaults
 *   (see gum::Configuration::diff()) or, if there are none, a note saying so followed by the whole
 *   configuration. This is what <tt>repr(pyagrum.config)</tt> shows.
 * - An unknown section or key raises a gum::SyntaxError; a value which does not respect the declared type
 *   raises a gum::InvalidArgument and nothing is changed.
 * - A <tt>float</tt> is written in decimal or scientific notation with a <tt>.</tt> (<tt>0.25</tt>,
 *   <tt>1e-6</tt>), whatever the locale of the program.
 * - gum::Configuration::get() returns a copy: the result stays valid whatever other threads do.
 *
 * A configuration can also be saved (gum::Configuration::save(): only the differences with the defaults are
 * written), restored (gum::Configuration::reset()), or stacked
 * (gum::Configuration::push() / gum::Configuration::pop()), which is handy in a test:
 * \code{.cpp}
 * auto& conf = gum::Configuration::instance();
 * conf.push();
 * conf.set("core", "jgumIndent", "2");
 * // ... code relying on this indentation ...
 * conf.pop();                                      // back to the previous values
 * \endcode
 *
 * # Hooks #
 *
 * A <em>hook</em> is a function registered with gum::Configuration::addHook() and called every time the
 * configuration changes. It is the way to make a value <em>act</em> on the library: the configuration
 * only stores values, a hook applies them.
 *
 * ## Registering a hook ##
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * gum::Configuration::instance().addHook([](const gum::Configuration& conf) {
 *   gum::setNumberOfThreads(static_cast< unsigned int >(
 *       conf.getTyped< std::int64_t >("core", "maxNumberOfThreads")));
 * });
 * \endcode
 *
 * The hook receives the configuration as a <tt>const</tt> reference. This is what the library does
 * itself for <tt>core.maxNumberOfThreads</tt> (see gum::Configuration::instance()), except that a
 * negative value (the default, -1) gives back the number of threads provided by the machine.
 *
 * ## When are hooks run ? ##
 *
 * Hooks are run, in the order of their registration,
 * - at the end of gum::Configuration::set() (unless its last argument <tt>noHook</tt> is <tt>true</tt>),
 * - at the end of gum::Configuration::reset(), gum::Configuration::pop() and
 *   gum::Configuration::load(),
 * - when gum::Configuration::runHooks() is called explicitly.
 *
 * <b>A hook is not called when it is registered</b>: if it must apply the current values immediately, call
 * gum::Configuration::runHooks() just after gum::Configuration::addHook() (hooks must be idempotent, as they
 * are called after <em>any</em> change, not only the one they are interested in). The library runs the hooks
 * once at start-up, after having loaded the user file.
 *
 * gum::Configuration::push() does not run the hooks (nothing changes).
 *
 * ## What a hook may (not) do ##
 *
 * - Hooks are run <em>outside</em> of any internal lock: a hook may freely read the configuration
 *   (<tt>get</tt>, <tt>getTyped</tt>...).
 * - A hook <b>must not change the configuration with a call that runs the hooks</b> (<tt>set</tt> without
 *   <tt>noHook = true</tt>, <tt>reset</tt>, <tt>pop</tt>, <tt>load</tt>): it would call itself endlessly. Use
 *   <tt>set(section, key, value, true)</tt> if it really has to correct a value.
 * - When a hook throws, the exception goes out of the call which triggered it (<tt>set</tt>,
 *   <tt>reset</tt>...); the new value <em>is</em> already stored and the hooks registered after the failing
 *   one are not run. A hook should therefore only throw for a real error.
 * - There is no way to unregister a hook: a hook captures only what is still alive for the whole life of
 *   the program (no reference to a local variable or to an object which can be destroyed).
 * - Hooks are shared by all the users of the singleton. In a test, prefer a local
 *   <tt>gum::Configuration conf(defaults_text)</tt> (its own, independent instance) to a hook on
 *   <tt>instance()</tt>.
 *
 * ## Hooks and threads ##
 *
 * Hooks may be called from any thread (the one which changed the configuration). A hook which touches shared
 * state must protect it (the number of threads of aGrUM, for instance, is an atomic).
 *
 * ## Hooks in pyAgrum ##
 *
 * <tt>pyagrum.config.add_hook(fn)</tt> registers a Python function (without argument) in the C++
 * configuration itself: it is called after each change, from Python or from C++, after the C++ hooks
 * registered before it. An exception raised by the function is propagated to whoever changed the
 * configuration.
 *
 * # Notices #
 *
 * Some events are not errors, but should not be silent: using a deprecated key, or finding an unknown key
 * or an invalid value in the user file (the valid part of the file is loaded anyway, the file itself is
 * never modified by gum::Configuration::load()). A deprecated key found in the user file is applied to its
 * replacement, so gum::Configuration::save() writes the file with the new key names. Only a malformed line
 * stops gum::Configuration::load() with a gum::SyntaxError: the whole file is read before anything is
 * applied, so nothing at all is changed in this case. The library never prints anything: these events are
 * queued (a notice already waiting is not queued twice), and gum::Configuration::takeNotices() returns and
 * empties the queue.
 * \code{.cpp}
 * for (const auto& notice: gum::Configuration::instance().takeNotices())
 *   std::cerr << notice.message << std::endl;
 * \endcode
 * pyAgrum turns them into Python warnings (<tt>DeprecationWarning</tt> for the deprecated keys).
 * As the user file is loaded when the library is loaded, the notices raised by this first loading are
 * waiting in the queue for the first call to gum::Configuration::takeNotices().
 *
 * # Using the configuration in a class of aGrUM #
 *
 * The usual idiom is a constructor with an explicit parameter, and a constructor (or a default argument)
 * which falls back on the configuration. For instance, the writers of the jgum format:
 * \code{.cpp}
 * gum::GumBNWriter< double > w1(false);      // indentation = core.jgumIndent
 * gum::GumBNWriter< double > w2(false, 2);   // indentation = 2, whatever the configuration
 * \endcode
 * The configuration is read <em>when the object is built</em>, not when it is used: changing the
 * configuration later does not change an existing object.
 *
 * To add a new key, add it to <tt>defaults.ini</tt> (with its metadata line, in the section of its module),
 * then read it where it is needed. No other change is required: sections and keys are not declared in C++.
 * A key which has to act on the library at start-up needs a hook (see above).
 *
 * # Several independent configurations #
 *
 * gum::Configuration can also be instantiated from any defaults text (its constructor takes the text, not a
 * file name). Such an instance is independent from the singleton, does not load any user file and has no
 * hook: this is what the unit tests use.
 * \code{.cpp}
 * gum::Configuration conf("[s]\n### int :: a number\nk = 1\n");
 * conf.set("s", "k", "5");
 * \endcode
 *
 * # Thread-safety #
 *
 * Every public method is thread-safe: reads (<tt>get</tt>, <tt>getTyped</tt>, <tt>diff</tt>...) run
 * concurrently, writes (<tt>set</tt>, <tt>load</tt>, <tt>push</tt>...) are exclusive.
 *
 * # See also #
 * - gum::Configuration for the complete API,
 * - <tt>pyagrum.config</tt> (and <tt>defaults.ini</tt>, which documents every key) on the pyAgrum side.
 */
